What the build takes: the font instanced at wght 400 (for Regular and Italic) and 700
(for Bold and Bold Italic) with its width axis kept (hebrew/build/instance.py), and from
each instance every glyph a code point the built face does not map reaches, every glyph
Noto's lookups name, and their components; Noto's dev2 and deva scripts in GSUB and GPOS
with their features and lookups; its mark filtering sets and glyph classes; its outline,
advance and anchor variations along the width axis. build/merge.py says how, and
baud-app/fonts/built/FONTLOG.txt what the built faces are.

The built faces' OFL.txt carries the copyright line above, nameID 0 carries it after
JetBrains Mono's and Cascadia's, and nameID 7 carries the font's trademark notice, "Noto
is a trademark of Google Inc.", after theirs: "Any trademark notices must remain in any
derivative fonts" (OFL-FAQ 1.1-update7, Q3.7).

Designers, per the font's nameID 8 and 9: Monotype Imaging Inc.; Jelle Bosma, Monotype
Design Team.

These are third-party glyphs in baud's font for the interim: baud's own Devanagari,
designed for the grid, is to replace them.
